{
  "feature_instruction": "This feature provides the client-side authentication mechanism for the Circuit House restaurant application, enabling users to log in as administrators and protecting admin routes. It consists of a React Context for global authentication state, a custom hook for interacting with the authentication API, a service for making API calls, TypeScript types for authentication data, a login page, and a protected route component.\n\n## Design Tokens\n- Navbar: bg-[#1A202C] text-white\n- Primary CTA: bg-[#D69E2E] hover:bg-[#B78725] text-white font-semibold rounded-full px-8 py-3 transition-all duration-200\n- Brand text accent: text-[#D69E2E]\n- Section bg: bg-[#F7FAFC] (odd sections) / bg-gray-50 (even sections)\n- Card: bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md border border-gray-100 p-6\n- Section container: <section className=\"py-16 px-4\"><div className=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto\">\n- Hero h1: text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold text-white\n- Body: text-[#2D3748] leading-relaxed\n\n### AuthContext.tsx\nThis file defines the `AuthContext` and `AuthProvider`. The `AuthContext` provides the current authentication state, including `isAuthenticated` (boolean), `user` (UserDto | null), `token` (string | null), and functions for `login` (which takes a token and user details) and `logout`. The `AuthProvider` component wraps the application and manages this state, storing the `token` in `localStorage` under the key 'token'. On initialization, it attempts to read the token and user from `localStorage` to restore the session.\n\n### auth.ts\nThis file defines the TypeScript interfaces for `AuthResponseDto`, `LoginRequestDto`, and `UserDto`. `LoginRequestDto` specifies the `username` and `password` fields required for login. `AuthResponseDto` contains the `token` and `user` details returned upon successful authentication. `UserDto` defines the structure of the authenticated user object, including `id`, `username`, and `roles`.\n\n### authService.ts\nThis service provides asynchronous functions to interact with the backend authentication API. It exports `loginUser(credentials: LoginRequestDto): Promise<AuthResponseDto>` which makes a POST request to `/api/auth/login`. It uses the shared `apiClient` from `frontend/src/api/client.ts` for HTTP requests.\n\n### useAuth.ts\nThis custom React hook, `useAuth()`, encapsulates the logic for authentication. It provides methods for `login` and `logout` using `react-query` mutations. The `login` function takes `LoginRequestDto` and on success, updates the `AuthContext` with the received `token` and `user` data. The `logout` function clears the `AuthContext` and removes the token from `localStorage`. It also provides `isAuthenticated` and `user` from the `AuthContext`.\n\n### LoginPage.tsx\nThis page (`/admin/login`) presents a login form for administrators. It uses the `useAuth` hook to handle the login process. Upon successful login, it redirects the user to the admin dashboard (`/admin/dashboard`). The form includes input fields for `username` and `password` and a submit button. The page layout is centered and styled according to the design tokens, using a clean, modern aesthetic with a focus on the 'Circuit House' brand.\n\n### ProtectedRoute.tsx\nThis component acts as a wrapper for routes that require authentication. It checks the `isAuthenticated` status from `AuthContext`. If the user is not authenticated, it redirects them to the `/admin/login` page. Otherwise, it renders the child components. This ensures that only logged-in administrators can access specific parts of the application, such as the admin dashboard and other admin-specific pages."
}
