{
  "feature_instruction": "## Design Tokens\n- Navbar: bg-[#1A202C] text-white\n- Primary CTA: bg-[#D69E2E] hover:bg-[#c08d2a] text-white font-semibold rounded-full px-8 py-3 transition-all duration-200\n- Brand text accent: text-[#D69E2E]\n- Section bg: bg-[#F7FAFC] (odd sections) / bg-white (even sections)\n- Card: bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md border border-gray-100 p-6\n- Section container: <section className=\"py-16 px-4\"><div className=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto\">\n- Hero h1: text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold text-white\n- Body: text-[#2D3748] leading-relaxed\n\nThis feature provides the complete frontend implementation for the online order and checkout flow for Circuit House, including public-facing cart management and checkout, as well as an admin interface for managing orders. It interacts with the `order-management` and `payment-processing` backend features.\n\n### `frontend/src/types/order.ts` and `frontend/src/types/payment.ts`\nThese files define the TypeScript interfaces for `OrderDto`, `OrderItemDto`, `CreateOrderRequest`, `OrderItemRequest`, `PaymentOrderRequest`, `PaymentVerificationRequest`, and `PaymentResponse` based on the backend API contracts from `order-management` and `payment-processing` features. These types are crucial for ensuring type safety across the frontend application when interacting with order and payment data.\n\n### `frontend/src/services/orderService.ts`\nThis service acts as the primary interface for making API calls related to orders and payments. It exports asynchronous functions `createOrder`, `createPaymentOrder`, `verifyPaymentSignature`, `getAllOrdersAdmin`, `getOrderByIdAdmin`, and `updateOrderStatusAdmin`.\n\n- `createOrder(orderRequest: CreateOrderRequest): Promise<OrderDto>`: Calls `POST /api/v1/orders` to create a new order.\n- `createPaymentOrder(paymentOrderRequest: PaymentOrderRequest): Promise<PaymentResponse>`: Calls `POST /api/v1/payments/orders` to initiate a payment order.\n- `verifyPaymentSignature(verificationRequest: PaymentVerificationRequest): Promise<string>`: Calls `POST /api/v1/payments/webhook` to verify a payment signature.\n- `getAllOrdersAdmin(): Promise<OrderDto[]>`: Calls `GET /api/v1/admin/orders` to fetch all orders for the admin panel.\n- `getOrderByIdAdmin(orderId: Long): Promise<OrderDto>`: Calls `GET /api/v1/admin/orders/{orderId}` to fetch a specific order by ID for the admin panel.\n- `updateOrderStatusAdmin(orderId: Long, newStatus: OrderStatus): Promise<OrderDto>`: Calls `PUT /api/v1/admin/orders/{orderId}/status` to update the status of an order.\n\n### `frontend/src/hooks/useOrders.ts`\nThis custom React hook leverages `react-query` to manage the state and side effects of order-related operations. It exposes mutations for creating orders and payments, and queries for fetching orders.\n\n- `useCreateOrder()`: Provides a mutation to call `orderService.createOrder`.\n- `useCreatePaymentOrder()`: Provides a mutation to call `orderService.createPaymentOrder`.\n- `useVerifyPaymentSignature()`: Provides a mutation to call `orderService.verifyPaymentSignature`.\n- `useGetAllOrdersAdmin()`: Provides a query to call `orderService.getAllOrdersAdmin`.\n- `useGetOrderByIdAdmin(orderId: Long)`: Provides a query to call `orderService.getOrderByIdAdmin`.\n- `useUpdateOrderStatusAdmin()`: Provides a mutation to call `orderService.updateOrderStatusAdmin`.\n\n### `frontend/src/context/CartContext.tsx`\nThis React Context manages the state of the user's shopping cart. It provides `cartItems`, `addItemToCart`, `removeItemFromCart`, `updateItemQuantity`, `clearCart`, and `cartTotal` to its consumers. `cartItems` is a `List<OrderItemDto>` and `cartTotal` is a `BigDecimal`.\n\n### `frontend/src/pages/OrderPage.tsx`\nThis page (`/order`) is the public-facing order and checkout flow. It uses the `CartContext` to display the `CartSummary`, and renders the `CheckoutForm` for customer details and payment initiation. After a successful order, it displays the `OrderConfirmation` component. The page is wrapped in the `Layout` component from `core-ui`.\n\nThe page will have the following sections:\n- **Cart Summary Section**: Displays the `CartSummary` component, showing items added to the cart, their quantities, and the calculated total. This section should have a clear heading like \"Your Order Summary\" and use `text-[#2D3748]` for body text.\n- **Checkout Form Section**: Displays the `CheckoutForm` component, where users input their details (name, email, phone) and initiate payment. This section should have a heading like \"Complete Your Order\" and include a primary CTA button for payment submission using the `Primary CTA` design token.\n- **Order Confirmation Section**: Conditionally displayed after a successful order, showing the `OrderConfirmation` component with a success message and order details. This section should have a prominent success message like \"Thank You for Your Order!\" and use `text-[#D69E2E]` for highlights.\n\n### `frontend/src/components/order/CartSummary.tsx`\nThis component displays the items currently in the `CartContext`. It shows each `MenuItemDto`'s name, quantity, price, and a subtotal. It also displays the overall `cartTotal`. Users can adjust item quantities or remove items from the cart using buttons that update the `CartContext`.\n\n### `frontend/src/components/order/CheckoutForm.tsx`\nThis form collects customer information (`customerName: string`, `customerEmail: string`, `customerPhone: string`, `notes: string`) and handles the order and payment initiation. It uses the `useCreateOrder` and `useCreatePaymentOrder` mutations from `useOrders.ts`. Upon successful order creation, it calls `createPaymentOrder` with the `orderId` and `totalAmount` from the created order. It then uses the `PaymentResponse` to open Razorpay's checkout interface. After payment, it calls `verifyPaymentSignature`.\n\n### `frontend/src/components/order/OrderConfirmation.tsx`\nThis component displays a confirmation message and the details of a successfully placed order. It receives an `OrderDto` as a prop and shows the order ID, total amount, and a list of `OrderItemDto`s. It should include a call to action to return to the home page or view order history.\n\n### `frontend/src/pages/AdminOrdersPage.tsx`\nThis page (`/admin/orders`) provides an administrative interface for viewing and managing all orders. It uses `useGetAllOrdersAdmin` from `useOrders.ts` to fetch all orders and displays them in an `OrdersTable`. It also integrates `OrderDetailModal` for viewing detailed order information and `useUpdateOrderStatusAdmin` for updating order statuses. The page is wrapped in the `AdminLayout` component from `core-ui`.\n\nThe page will have the following sections:\n- **Admin Orders Header**: A clear heading like \"Manage Customer Orders\" using `text-[#1A202C]`.\n- **Orders Table Section**: Displays the `OrdersTable` component, listing all orders. This table should allow for sorting and filtering, and each row should have actions to view details or update status.\n- **Order Detail Modal**: Conditionally renders the `OrderDetailModal` when an admin clicks to view a specific order's details.\n\n### `frontend/src/components/admin/orders/OrdersTable.tsx`\nThis component displays a paginated and sortable table of `OrderDto` objects. Each row should show key order details like `orderId`, `userId`, `totalAmount`, `orderStatus`, and `orderDate`. It provides actions to view `OrderDetailModal` for a specific order and to update the `orderStatus` using `useUpdateOrderStatusAdmin`.\n\n### `frontend/src/components/admin/orders/OrderDetailModal.tsx`\nThis modal dialog displays the complete details of a single `OrderDto`. It shows all fields of the `OrderDto`, including the list of `OrderItemDto`s. It also allows an admin to update the `orderStatus` using `useUpdateOrderStatusAdmin` and provides a close button.\n",
